      "content": "It is often helpful for students to view \"progressive\" ideas as the engine of human growth and flexibility, while \"regressive\" ideas act as barriers that resist necessary change. Creating a clear distinction allows students to visualize history as a timeline where advocacy for justice pushes society forward, whereas defending the status quo often stunts it. However, it is important to acknowledge that what is considered \"progress\" can sometimes be nuanced, so framing the lesson around \"advocating for dignity\" versus \"enforcing oppression\" provides the clearest moral guidance.",
